Z||000001||Phil||11-12-2001||08:20 AM||||Are you after a tool to ease the tension on the idler so that you can remove the belt? If that is the case - there is no special tool, you just put the proper size wrench on the pulley nut and turn.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jeffndebrus||11-12-2001||09:13 AM||||no, i need a belt tensioning tool described as a turnbuckle arrangement that goes between the flywheel pulley and the alternator or a/c pulley and by turning the large center nut it expznds the tool thereby tightening the belt so you can easily adjust belt tension and tighten bolts without having to use both arms and both legs.<BR>after your belt is tightened you simply remove the tool and off you go.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||11-12-2001||09:36 AM||||J.C.Whitney  about $7  or as someone suggested on the Forum awhile back - use a large set of water pump pliers on the bolt boss and the end of the bracket.||||reg||1
